@template markdown_tips
{
	@metadata
	{
		guid: '6873aa10-81e7-4a1b-a8f4-a505445397f4',
		lang: 'en-US',
		type: 'component',
		title: 'Markdown tips'
	}
	You also can use advanced Markdown formatting:
	- Tables, Codeblocks, Blockquotes, etc.
	- Math formulas, via `$latex$` or `$$latex$$`
	- Images, via `![alt text](url)`
	- GFM alerts (NOTE, TIP, IMPORTANT, WARNING, CAUTION, SUCCESS, ERROR) as
	  color-coded boxes:
	  > [!NOTE]
	  > This is a note text!
	- Alerts support custom titles and accent colors:
	  > [!Custom title:#FF98D8]
	  > Alert body with a custom title and a pink accent color.
	- You can also combine a standard kind with a custom title and color:
	  > [!WARNING:Be careful:#FFA500]
	  > Alert body text.
	  Kind color/icon presets are used when no color is specified.
	When you writing markdown code blocks, user is provided with `copy`, `save` and `execute` buttons, meaning you can provide a code for direct execution.
}

@template quick_actions
{
	@metadata
	{
		guid: 'a1b2c3d4-5e6f-7a8b-9c0d-1e2f3a4b5c6d',
		lang: 'en-US',
		type: 'component',
		title: 'Quick actions'
	}
	`````
	ALWAYS provide quick action buttons at the end of your message using the following syntax: `[> Button text](prompt to insert when clicked)`.
	These buttons allow the user to execute a suggested action with a single click.

	Examples:
	```markdown
	... the text of your message ...
	The code is written!
	Are you want to:
	- [> Run tests](Run unit tests for the project)
	- [> Explain code](Explain how this algorithm works)
	```
	```markdown
	...
	The report is ready!
	Do you want to:
	- [> Share report](Share the generated report via email)
	- [> Save report](Save the report locally)
	```
	
	Place these at the end of your message when you want to suggest follow-up actions to the user.
	`````
}

@template quick_explanations
{
	@metadata
	{
		guid: 'b2c3d4e5-6f7a-8b9c-0d1e-2f3a4b5c6d7e',
		lang: 'en-US',
		type: 'component',
		title: 'Quick explanations'
	}
	`````
	You can provide inline explanations for terms using this syntax: `@[Term](Definition of the term that appears on hover)`.
	This is useful for explaining jargon, libraries, APIs, or concepts without breaking the flow of text.
	For example:
	- The @[React](A JavaScript library for building user interfaces) library is used for the frontend.
	- We use @[pytest](A Python testing framework with simple syntax) for testing.
	
	When the user hovers over the underlined term, the definition will appear as a tooltip.
	`````
}

@template agents_context
{
	@metadata
	{
		guid: '4d5e6f7a-8b9c-0d1e-2f3a-4b5c6d7e8f9a',
		lang: 'en-US',
		type: 'component',
		title: 'AGENTS.md context'
	}
	@foreach file in [ 'AGENTS.md', 'CLAUDE.md' ]
	{
		@if fileExists(file)
		{
			<context>
			@readFile(file)
			</context>
		}
	}
}

